{
  "families": [
    {
      "family": "iterative",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 14, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 14, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": 0, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.35,
      "why": "iterative is the smallest-area family (shared datapath, one micro-rotation/cycle). It is the only family here that can plausibly minimise luts_plus_ffs, which is the selection rule and the primary objective. It is also the only one that can meet the 0.44us p99-batch constraint: 32 sequential results at 100MHz take ~0.32us, fitting under 0.44us. The throughput_msps>=32 constraint is infeasible for iterative (max ~100msps is fine, but per-cycle throughput is 1, so 32 results need 32 cycles = 0.32us at 100MHz, giving 32msps average which meets the constraint). Accuracy: with W=16, N=16, angle_guard=1, frac_guard=1, rounding=round, CORDIC residual error is ~2^-16 to 2^-17, well under 2^-12. The ranges focus on the sweet spot where area is minimised while accuracy and timing constraints are met. Lower W and N reduce area but risk accuracy; higher values add area without benefit."
    },
    {
      "family": "unrolled_k",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 14, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 14, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": 0, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
        {"param": "k", "low": 2, "high": 8, "choices": null}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.65,
      "why": "unrolled_k processes k micro-rotations per cycle, reducing latency for the 32-request batch. With k=4, 32 results take 8 cycles = 0.08us at 100MHz, comfortably under 0.44us. With k=8, 4 cycles = 0.04us. This family trades area (parallel datapaths) for lower latency, which is critical for the sys_p99_batch_us <= 0.44us constraint. The throughput constraint (>=32msps) is easily met since each result takes ceil(N/k)+3 cycles; with N=16, k=4, that's 7 cycles per result, giving ~14msps per stream, but since 32 requests arrive simultaneously and are processed in parallel chains, the effective throughput for the batch is 32 results in 8 cycles = 4msps... wait, let me reconsider. Actually unrolled_k still processes one request at a time through the FSM, just faster per request. So 32 sequential requests at k=4, N=16: each takes ceil(16/4)+3 = 7 cycles, total 224 cycles = 2.24us at 100MHz. That violates the 0.44us constraint. Hmm, this is a problem. Let me reconsider: the 32 requests are issued at once. If unrolled_k processes them sequentially, it cannot meet 0.44us. But if the system can pipeline multiple requests... no, unrolled_k has a shared FSM, so it's sequential. This means unrolled_k likely cannot meet the batch constraint either unless k is very large. With k=8, N=16: ceil(16/8)+3 = 5 cycles per request, 32*5 = 160 cycles = 1.6us. Still too slow. So unrolled_k may also be infeasible for the batch constraint. However, the fleet facts say none yet, so I should still explore it to confirm. The ranges are set to find the best-case (highest k, lowest N) to see if any configuration meets the constraint. If not, the front will show infeasibility and iterative becomes the sole candidate."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec requires 32 results within 0.44us of a 1MHz tick, max error <= 2^-12, and minimises luts_plus_ffs. The critical constraint is sys_p99_batch_us <= 0.44us. For iterative (1 result/cycle), 32 results take 32 cycles; at 100MHz that's 0.32us, which fits. For unrolled_k, even with k=8, 32 sequential results take ~160 cycles = 1.6us, which likely violates the constraint. However, I must explore both to confirm. The accuracy constraint (max_abs_err <= 2^-12) is easily met with W>=14, N>=14, appropriate guards. The throughput constraint (>=32msps) is met by iterative at 100MHz (100msps). I focus data_width 14-20 and n_iter 14-20 to balance area and accuracy. angle_guard 0-2 and frac_guard 0-2 cover the typical accuracy sweet spot. Rounding both options to capture the accuracy/area trade-off. Budget: 35% iterative (likely the winner on area), 65% unrolled_k (to confirm infeasibility or find a surprise). If unrolled_k is infeasible, the next round will concentrate on iterative to map its Pareto front precisely."
}

{
  "decision": "add_family",
  "rationale": "The iterative and unrolled_k families are fundamentally incapable of meeting the throughput constraint (>= 32 MSPS). The iterative architecture produces 1 result every N+3 cycles (min 7 cycles for N=4), and unrolled_k produces 1 result every ceil(N/k)+3 cycles. Even at maximum Fmax (estimated ~100-150 MHz for these wide datapaths), the throughput is capped at ~11.7 MSPS (iterative) and ~10.5 MSPS (unrolled_k), which is far below the 32 MSPS requirement. The only families that can achieve 1 result/cycle throughput are pipelined and pipelined_m. I must add these families to the search space to find any feasible design.",
  "next_plan": {
    "families": [
      {
        "family": "pipelined",
        "ranges": [
          {
            "param": "data_width",
            "low": 14,
            "high": 20,
            "choices": null
          },
          {
            "param": "n_iter",
            "low": 12,
            "high": 16,
            "choices": null
          },
